From 6ae96c66f0cd620cb9c36dd395902584b2a7d53a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hotty Agent <hotty@neuroplast.io>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 10:46:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 31/45] blitz-dom: an inline box's margin, border and padding
 take their space

A non-atomic inline box (a span in a line of text) went into its inline
root's parley layout as a style span only, so its inline-start and
inline-end margin, border and padding took no space:
`A<i style="margin-left:2ch">x</i>` drew "Ax".

Construction now puts a zero-height parley inline box at the start and
the end of the element's style span for each edge that has a margin,
border or padding; its id is the element's node id with a flag bit set.
Inline layout sizes it, resolving percentages against the inline root's
content width, and puts it in flow, so parley breaks, aligns and
reorders it as it does an atomic inline. It is built out-of-flow, since
parley's builder flushes collapsible white space before an in-flow box,
and an edge of no width stays out-of-flow, adding no break opportunity.
A restyle that adds or removes an edge rebuilds the inline layout; one
that only resizes it lays it out again. Client rects count the
element's border and padding but not its margin.

This is the spacing only: blitz-paint still draws an inline box's
background behind its glyph runs, and draws no inline borders. And as
parley takes an edge for an atomic inline, a line can break at an edge
inside a word, an edge takes the bidi level of the run before it, and a
negative margin cancels the edge's space but overlaps nothing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
